WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.992
An import goes wrong, but business does not stop.

00:00:02.992 --> 00:00:05.985
New invoices arrive. Payments are recorded. Someone fixes a

00:00:05.985 --> 00:00:08.977
few prices by hand. Restoring yesterday's backup would erase

00:00:08.977 --> 00:00:11.970
that later work. Rewind helps you undo the import

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:13.632
and keep what came afterward.

00:00:14.132 --> 00:00:17.401
I start with three versions: the last good export,

00:00:17.401 --> 00:00:20.670
a checkpoint just after the mistake, and today's file.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:23.940
The checkpoint lets Rewind separate damaged prices from observed

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:27.209
later edits. CSV exports and small SQLite snapshots are

00:00:27.209 --> 00:00:27.572
supported.

00:00:28.092 --> 00:00:31.315
I describe the mistake: the import multiplied our prices

00:00:31.315 --> 00:00:34.538
by one hundred. Vultr Serverless Inference writes an investigation

00:00:34.538 --> 00:00:37.761
that reads the three copies in its own gVisor

00:00:37.761 --> 00:00:40.984
environment. The controller checks the proposed cells against
separate

00:00:40.984 --> 00:00:41.700
trusted inspections.

00:00:42.212 --> 00:00:45.420
The investigation finds one hundred seventeen prices to restore.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.628
Three prices changed again after the mistake, so they

00:00:48.628 --> 00:00:51.837
are protected. I can uncheck individual cells. Approval applies

00:00:51.837 --> 00:00:54.332
only to the exact cells I select.

00:00:54.852 --> 00:00:58.215
Before approving, I review the complete repair. A whole-backup

00:00:58.215 --> 00:01:01.577
replacement would remove seven newer invoices and reset twenty-
three

00:01:01.577 --> 00:01:04.940
updates. Rewind keeps all one hundred twenty-seven records and

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:08.302
every value outside the repair. I approve this exact

00:01:08.302 --> 00:01:08.676
plan.

00:01:09.176 --> 00:01:12.547
Vultr AI writes the approved recovery. It runs on

00:01:12.547 --> 00:01:15.917
disposable copies in a separate container, without network access

00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:19.288
or controller credentials. A fresh trusted worker reads the

00:01:19.288 --> 00:01:22.659
output. The controller checks every approved replacement and every

00:01:22.659 --> 00:01:23.408
protected cell.

00:01:23.908 --> 00:01:27.130
One hundred seventeen prices are restored. All one hundred

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:30.353
twenty-seven invoices remain. Seven newer records, twenty
payments, and

00:01:30.353 --> 00:01:33.575
three later price edits are kept. The approved replacements

00:01:33.575 --> 00:01:36.798
match the good version. Every other cell matches today's

00:01:36.798 --> 00:01:37.156
file.

00:01:37.668 --> 00:01:40.655
Here are the three overlapping prices. You can see

00:01:40.655 --> 00:01:43.642
the good value, the value after the mistake, and

00:01:43.642 --> 00:01:46.629
today's value. Today's price wins. Rewind leaves those cells

00:01:46.629 --> 00:01:49.284
alone, preserving the work done after the import.

00:01:49.788 --> 00:01:53.676
Both model requests used Vultr Inference. Seven isolated runs

00:01:53.676 --> 00:01:57.564
completed, including the investigation, recovery, and separate
result inspection.

00:01:57.564 --> 00:02:01.452
The data checks passed, the original files stayed unchanged,

00:02:01.452 --> 00:02:05.340
and every temporary container was removed before download became

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:05.772
available.

00:02:06.272 --> 00:02:09.687
I download the recovered CSV and complete change list.

00:02:09.687 --> 00:02:13.102
The evidence includes the approved plan, file fingerprints, real

00:02:13.102 --> 00:02:16.517
model response identifiers, and confirmed container removal.
Rewind returns

00:02:16.517 --> 00:02:19.933
a checked copy. The operator decides when to apply

00:02:19.933 --> 00:02:20.312
it.

00:02:20.832 --> 00:02:23.715
This separate containment check ran on the same Vultr

00:02:23.715 --> 00:02:26.598
worker. A program tries to keep running. The sandbox

00:02:26.598 --> 00:02:29.481
stops it at the two-second deadline and verifies that

00:02:29.481 --> 00:02:32.364
its container is gone. Recovery tests also rejected a

00:02:32.364 --> 00:02:35.247
whole-backup replacement and a blind price reset that would

00:02:35.247 --> 00:02:36.528
erase the later edits.
